@prefix orexis: <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix execution: <http://example.org/orexis/execution#> .
@prefix planning: <http://example.org/orexis/planning#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix sh:   <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/planning#> .
@prefix prediction: <http://example.org/orexis/prediction#> .

<> a orexis:OntologyGraph .    # this document is one graph, and this is what it is

#  OREXIS PLANNING.
#  The top layer's own words (#529): what a pass FINDS — a plan, and the want it was found for —
#  what the derivations read and write, and the scopes that partition follows. What every layer
#  and every package writes in stays `orexis:`; a term one layer reads and writes is that layer's,
#  and its prefix says so.  FEWER WORDS THAN THE PREDECESSOR'S FIFTY-FOUR, and never counted here.
#  What is gone is everything a pass RECORDED about itself for a reader — the candidates it
#  weighed, what each world would reach, how long it took, what it chose and why — and the
#  remembered plan hung on a want. Both were read by code this layer no longer has, and a term
#  nobody reads is annotation: they come back with their readers and not before.




planning:DerivationGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:PublicGraph , orexis:BeliefGraph ; rdfs:label "Derivation graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What every loaded package's derivation rules READ and WRITE, one `planning:Derivation` per INSERT, written whole at every refresh of public knowledge. The scope partition joins predicates wherever one action OR one derivation touches both, and the actions are in the store while the rules are texts on disk — so the edges the rules make are put in the store beside them, and `scope_actions` is a function of the store alone. PUBLIC, because the loaded rule set is the same for everyone reading this store, exactly as the actions graph is; computed, so it is cleared and rewritten rather than added to. A BELIEF graph, as the vocabulary graph is and for the same reason: what a loaded rule reads and writes is a fact about this build, believed, and a public graph that named no modality would leave a reader guessing at its content." .
planning:Derivation a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Derivation" ;
    rdfs:comment "One INSERT of one loaded derivation rule, as the edge it makes: the predicates its WHERE reads and the predicates its template writes. Named for the rule file it came from and its place in it, so the same rules write the same text. A side whose predicates cannot be read — a variable predicate, a text the parser refuses — says `planning:Anything` and joins every predicate there is, since a rule that might touch anything cannot be proven not to." .
planning:reads a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain planning:Derivation ; rdfs:label "reads" ;
    rdfs:comment "A predicate this derivation's WHERE reads, or `planning:Anything` where the text does not say." .
planning:writes a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain planning:Derivation ; rdfs:label "writes" ;
    rdfs:comment "A predicate this derivation's template writes, or `planning:Anything` where the text does not say." .
planning:Anything a owl:Thing ; rdfs:label "Anything" ;
    rdfs:comment "Said where a side's predicates cannot be read off the text: the derivation might read or write any predicate, so it joins them all. `touches`' own ANYTHING, in the store." .

##  What the loaded derivations read and write — computed at every refresh of public knowledge
##  from the rule files, and read by `scope_actions`, which names no file.
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/derivations> a planning:DerivationGraph ;
    orexis:arrivedBy orexis:Derived .

planning:PlanGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:WorkingGraph ; rdfs:label "Plan graph" ;
    rdfs:comment """ONE WANT'S PLAN — what a pass decided, as a graph of its own, in the imaginarium beside the worlds it decided among. One per want and replaced whole, which is the first reason it is a graph rather than a corner of the pass graph: replacing a plan is clearing this, where before it was enumerating the names a plan's steps MIGHT have taken and removing those, a step count the writer had to guess at.

The second reason is the crossing. A plan goes to the execution layer to be carried out, and a graph is what `prepare_ground` showed the shape of: one function over two stores, copying what the other side needs. Copying a plan is then copying a graph, rather than walking `execution:then` and hoping the walk reaches everything a step carries.

A WORKING graph, and memory: the imaginarium is not on disk. A plan is a pass's result and not yet a commitment; what survives is the intention the execution layer mints when it copies one in (`the executor's adoption`).""" .

planning:PossibleGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:WorkingGraph ; rdfs:label "Possible graph" ;
    rdfs:comment """ONE NODE OF A SEARCH — what the world comes to if the steps reaching it were taken — in the imaginarium, beside the ground it was forked from and the other worlds the pass imagined. Never mutated: siblings are alive at the same time, so branching rather than backtracking is the hard case, and a world is a VALUE written once when the node is created.

WHERE IT CAME FROM IS IN THE STORE, AS AN EDGE. It says which candidate reached it (`planning:by`), and the candidate says which world it was taken in (`planning:from`) — so a candidate CONNECTS two possible worlds, and the tree a pass walked is read by walking `planning:by/planning:from`. It was in the graph's NAME — a path of action segments, `<parent>.<action>-<values>` — which is a reader depending on a spelling, and a graph's name is for eyes; the name is the mint number alone since #486, `possible/<n>`, the same length at any depth, and the path is read off the rows.

WHAT IS TRUE OF IT WHOEVER ASKS is written on its row: what the path to it spent (`planning:spent`), when it is (its `dcterms:temporal` period: the earliest and the latest instant the landings on its path reach it, one instant where every landing is exact), the mint number of the candidate that made it, which is its name (`planning:minted`), and what it holds (`orexis:hash`). What a want's search worked out about it — met here, on the frontier, opened — is a WEIGHING's, one per want, because one imaginarium holds the worlds of every want in a scope and a world has as many askers as there are wants.

A WORKING graph, and memory: an imaginarium is not on disk, and a possible world is what a pass considered rather than anything the agent believes.""" .

planning:by a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain planning:PossibleGraph ; rdfs:range planning:Candidate ;
    rdfs:label "by" ;
    rdfs:comment "The candidate whose effect made this world out of the one it was taken in — the other half of the edge whose first half is the candidate's `planning:from`, so it is followable from either end. A ground says nothing here: what makes a ground is a prediction nobody takes, and its own period says when." .

planning:from a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain planning:Candidate ; rdfs:range orexis:Graph ;
    rdfs:label "from" ;
    rdfs:comment "The world this candidate would be taken IN — a possible world, or the ground a search stands in. THE CANDIDATE'S, not the world's: a candidate the search weighed and passed over reaches no world at all, so it is named for the world it leaves, and an edge between two worlds is the candidate that joins them." .

planning:spent a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "spent" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the path to this world cost — and, on a plan, what the path to the world it reaches cost, which is what the pass scored the plan to spend — each step's `planning:costs` summed, in the unit the search spends. Its own word rather than the action's, which is a SPARQL text to evaluate where this is a number the path accumulated. The ground spent nothing and says nothing; a reader reads its absence as nought. THE FRONTIER IS ORDERED BY IT, which is what lets the open list be a query rather than a heap." .

planning:minted a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "minted" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where this candidate came in the order the passes over one store admitted candidates — and, on a possible world, the number of the candidate that made it, carried on: an edge and the world at its end are one thing said twice, so one number serves both. THE NAME, since a candidate's and a world's name is this number and nothing else (#486), and THE TIE-BREAK, not decoration: candidates are taken in this order, and two worlds of equal cost are opened in it, which is what makes a uniform-cost search go layer by layer rather than dive alphabetically into whichever action sorts first. The counter is the store's — the highest number anything in it carries — so a cone kept across passes is never named over. It was the heap's counter." .

planning:Weighing a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Weighing" ;
    rdfs:comment "WHAT A PASS WORKED OUT ABOUT ONE WORLD FOR ONE WANT — OR FOR ONE DESIRE IN ONE GROUND, which is the same judgment at the other grain: a ground is a world, a desire is what a want is an instance of, and the met-test's report in that world is what both read. The search reads whether the report has any row; the derivation reads the rows, since a desire is universal and its violations are the instances in trouble, and reads them across the grounds, since a ground holds over a period and successive grounds give a violation its stretch. One node for both, written by one function, because two would be the same shape twice — the relationship between the two, reified because it is true of neither alone: a world's own facts hold whoever asks, but whether a want is met there, whether the search may still open it and whether it has, are true of the world AND the want together. One imaginarium holds the worlds of every want in a scope, so a world has as many of these as there are wants asking about it, and writing them on the world would have been a world pretending to have one asker. A CANDIDATE the search passed over is weighed the same way, so a want whose search was offered nothing can be told apart from one whose levers all led somewhere already seen. THE FRONTIER IS THESE ROWS: the search state a pass needs is here and nowhere in Python, which is what lets a pass be continued." .

planning:weighs a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain planning:Weighing ;
    rdfs:label "weighs" ;
    rdfs:comment "What this weighing is about — a possible WORLD, the GROUND the search stands in, or a CANDIDATE the search passed over. Said in one direction, from the weighing, exactly as `planning:for` is." .

planning:violation a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain planning:Weighing ;
    rdfs:label "violation" ;
    rdfs:comment "One row of the met-test's report in the world weighed: which instance (`planning:instance`), which constraint of the shape (`planning:constraint`, the constraint's index within it), and what that constraint is about (`planning:about`), where the shape's block says it. A weighing with none, and `planning:met true`, is a world the want or desire is met in; one with none and NO `planning:met` is a question never put — the met-test could not be compiled or run — which is not the same as met." .
planning:instance a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:label "instance" ;
    rdfs:comment "The focus node a violation is about — the instance in trouble, which is the grain a want is minted at." .
planning:constraint a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ; rdfs:label "constraint" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which constraint of the met-test a violation is of, as the compiler numbers them within the shape — with the instance, one WAY of failing, which is what a want's stretch is read for across the grounds." .
planning:offending a rdf:Property ; rdfs:label "offending" ;
    rdfs:comment "The value that made a violation, where its constraint has one — SHACL's value node: the reading that is below, the level that is five. A node or a literal. The report's row says it, so the derivation can read off the world what the reading in trouble is keyed by — its feature, the bed — and place a witness whose focus node is the agent in the scope of the bed it is about (a-scope-is-a-predicate-on-a-key, two instances). A count constraint has none." .
planning:keyedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain planning:Want ; rdfs:label "keyed by" ;
    rdfs:comment "A term of the key of what this want is about — the bed whose reading is in trouble — written by the derivation where the key, and not what the want is about, is what placed the witness in its scope. The one row a want states beside its met-test, because the shape cannot say it: the desire targets the agent and reaches the reading by a path, and a second bed's reading comes down the same path. The Planner places a want by the predicates its met-test reads, then by the terms it names, this among them. Never a property: what may repair a want is the planning problem, and a want stating one had answered it before the planner was asked." .

planning:remaining a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain planning:Weighing ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "remaining" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the want's `planning:estimates` reads IN this world — how far it still is, in the unit the search spends, never overstated. With the world's `planning:spent` it is the A* key the frontier is ordered by, and the sum is what the cheapest achiever bounds: a world whose spent and remaining reach what the achiever spent is not opened. A want declaring no estimate reads nought, and the search is uniform-cost. The predecessor's `deliberation:remaining`, the word kept." .

planning:met a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain planning:Weighing ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "met" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether the want's met-test passes in the world weighed. The verdict of one judgment, written where it was decided so that nobody judges the same world for the same want twice — a pass resumed here, or a pass for another want sharing the world. ABSENT MEANS THE QUESTION WAS NEVER PUT, which is not the same as no: a candidate passed over has no world to judge — or that the world is IMPOSSIBLE (`planning:impossible` on its row), where the question is moot and the verdict is taken back, so the weighing is neither on the frontier nor an achiever by its rows." .

planning:open a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain planning:Weighing ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "open" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether the world weighed is on the frontier — somewhere this want's search may still open. THE OPEN LIST IS THESE ROWS, ordered by what the world spent and then by the order it was minted; an iteration needs nothing carried over from the last one but the name the query hands back. TAKEN BACK when the world is opened, or when a constraint makes it impossible (`planning:impossible`), and never written for a world that met the want or a candidate that reached nothing: a settled world is not on the frontier, so its absence is said rather than deduced from `planning:expanded`." .

planning:expanded a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain planning:Weighing ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "expanded" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether this want's search has opened the world weighed — its candidates asked for and every one of them weighed. Written when the last is, so a world the budget cut short stays open and a resumed pass takes up the candidates it had not reached, which it tells by the weighings that stand." .

planning:repeats a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain planning:Weighing ; rdfs:range orexis:Graph ;
    rdfs:label "repeats" ;
    rdfs:comment "Said of a weighing of a CANDIDATE that reached no new world: the world its fork came to holds exactly what this one already held, by hash — its parent's, where the effect changed nothing, or an earlier world's, where the path went round. A cycle is a world already seen, and this is where the search says it saw one." .

planning:impossible a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain planning:PossibleGraph ; rdfs:range planning:Constraint ;
    rdfs:label "impossible" ;
    rdfs:comment "Said of a possible world in which a `planning:Constraint` the holder holds is violated — the constraint named — so the world is one the search may not stand in, whatever any want's met-test says there. ON THE WORLD'S ROW and not on a weighing, since a world's possibility is true of it whoever asks, where a weighing is one want's. The weighing of any want in such a world is BARE — `planning:for` and `planning:weighs` and no verdict, `planning:open` and `planning:met` taken back where they were written before the constraint was weighed — so the frontier's read and the plan's pass it by by their rows alone, with no filter on either: a filter inside the frontier's read cost every holder a quarter of its pass, measured (#902). The constraint's own weighing stands beside with its rows, so what the world violates can be read off the store. Kept and never forgotten, since the candidate that reached the world is then weighed and a resumed pass does not take it again. A ground is never marked: the present is validated and said, not repaired, and a want is searched from it as it stands." .

planning:Candidate a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Candidate" ;
    rdfs:comment """A MOVE A WORLD ADMITS — one action and one filling of it, off the action's own `planning:precondition`, whose rows ARE the fillings. A search walks possible worlds and forks on a candidate at a time; the ones it PICKS become the plan's steps, and that is the whole difference between the two words. They were one Python class until the fields that would have told them apart turned out to be set by nothing.

ONE PER POSSIBLE WORLD, written beside the world's row when the fork is made, so the chain a search walked is in the store rather than in a tuple a node happened to carry. `extract_plan` reads it back. NAMED BY ITS MINT NUMBER, `possible/<n>.by`, and the world it makes is `possible/<n>` — one spelling apart, for eyes; what it fills and where it leaves from are its rows, and no reader parses the name.""" .

planning:fills a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range orexis:Action ;
    rdfs:label "fills" ;
    rdfs:comment """The action template a candidate — or the step it became — is a filling of. NO DOMAIN, because it is said of both: a step IS a candidate that was picked, and what it fills does not change in the picking.

IT IS PLANNING'S WORD, on a node the intentions also hold. Execution reads a step's membership (`execution:partOf`) and its order (`execution:then`) and nothing else of it, so what it FILLS is the search's fact about it and carries the search's prefix. It was `execution:fills` beside this one, two spellings of one relationship separated only by whether the search had picked yet — which is the synonym the dictionary refuses, and it was written by planning and read by no line of execution.""" .

planning:of a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range planning:Candidate ;
    rdfs:label "of" ;
    rdfs:comment "The candidate a step was minted from — the one link between what the search walked and what the plan holds. Execution does not read it: a commitment is what an agent is doing, not how it was found. It crosses with the rest because what a lower layer does not read it also does not drop." .

planning:GroundGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:WorkingGraph ; rdfs:label "Ground graph" ;
    rdfs:comment """WHAT THE AGENT'S OWN KNOWLEDGE COMES TO OVER ONE PERIOD — the present, or what a prediction made of the period before it — materialised in an imaginarium as one graph, classified with the stretch it holds over. Which ground holds at an instant is then the door's question and no reader is handed a list.

IT EXISTS BECAUSE A PREDICTION SUPERSEDES. A prediction is a graph holding the reading it foretells, and a door handed a reader every graph holding at an instant — so a met-test asked at a foreseen instant saw the reading AND the prediction of it, and a shape holds over every value, so the stale one still violated. A tank low now with a forecast refilling it read as unmet for ever. Nothing was wrong with the forecast; there was no place in the pass where it REPLACED anything. A prediction's graph is what it ADDS, its catalogue row says when it applies and carries `orexis:retracts` — a `DELETE … WHERE` naming `GRAPH $state`, run against a fork of the period before, and what the prediction holds is added after it. The adds are concrete because a forecast is; only the retraction is a pattern, because what is standing in that place is not something a forecast can name in advance.

NEIGHBOURS THAT HASH THE SAME ARE ONE. Each ground carries the same hash a possible world does, and where two periods reach the same facts the second is not a period: nothing a met-test can read moved, so nothing it could answer differs. That is what bounds the judging — a desire is asked once per distinct ground rather than once per boundary.

A WORKING graph, and memory: an imaginarium is not on disk, and a ground is what a pass stands on rather than anything the agent believes.""" .

planning:ScopeGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:WorkingGraph ; rdfs:label "Scope graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "THE STORE's scopes — nobody's, and one graph rather than one per agent, since every agent reading one store would compute the same partition from the same rows — written whole by `scope_actions` from the actions the store holds and the derivations loaded: which atoms — a predicate on a key — some one filling of one action reads or writes together, as `planning:Scope` nodes, and which scope each predicate, each term a key holds and each action is in. The partition is a function of the actions loaded, which do not change while the agent runs; it was recomputed in Python on every derivation and is data now, so `derive_wants` reads it and clusters the results by it. A working graph: a conclusion, replaced whole per run, carried into no possible world and read beside no state, since none of it is a fact about the world a shape is written against." .
##  The store's scopes — written whole by `scope_actions` from the actions the store holds and
##  the derivations loaded, and read by `derive_wants`. Declared here because it is named: one
##  graph for the store, belonging to no agent, so there is no id to build it from.
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/scopes> a planning:ScopeGraph ;
    orexis:arrivedBy orexis:Derived .

planning:Scope a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Scope" ;
    rdfs:comment "A set of atoms — a predicate on a key, the public terms a filling binds the subject by — joined wherever one filling of one action reads or writes both, and separate where nothing does — a bulkhead in the vocabulary (knowledge/domain/planning/scope.md). Two wants in different scopes cannot contradict, because no action of one writes a fact the other reads. Named for the graph it is written in and its place in the partition, largest first." .
planning:inScope a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range planning:Scope ; rdfs:label "in scope" ;
    rdfs:comment "Every scope a predicate, a term or an action is in: a predicate in every scope some filling writes it in, a term in every scope a filling binding it falls in, an action in every scope some filling of it lies in. Said in full, never only where it is one: a reading, a witness and a want are placed by the MEET of their terms' scopes, and a bed keyed by a pump's filling and a heater's is in both scopes, which is what tells its soil's reading from another bed's — said only where one, it was in neither and told nothing (a-scope-is-a-predicate-on-a-key). A reader wanting the term unique to a scope asks for one scope. No domain: said of a property, of a term a key holds and of an action alike. An action stating no effect is in no scope, as no world admits it; one whose effect cannot be read is in the one scope that joins everything." .

planning:Plan a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Plan" ;
    rdfs:comment "WHAT ONE PASS FOUND, written down: the steps it would take, in order, and what they spent. One per want, named for it and replaced whole, in the imaginarium beside the worlds it was decided among. ITS STEPS ARE HELD IN THE WORDS OF WHOEVER READS THEM: `execution:Step`, chained by `execution:then` and held together by `execution:partOf`, because the executor adopts membership and order as they stand. What each step FILLS and which candidate it came from are the search's facts about it and carry the search's prefix." .

planning:outcome a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain planning:Plan ; rdfs:range planning:Outcome ;
    rdfs:label "outcome" ;
    rdfs:comment "Why the pass ended, which is the whole reason a plan with no steps is written down at all: an empty plan is an ANSWER and this says which of the three it is. It was a field on a Python object the pass returned and then dropped, so the finding a want most needed — that nothing this agent holds points at it — was the one thing nothing outside the process could read." .

planning:Outcome a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Outcome" ;
    rdfs:comment "How one pass over one want ended. Three, and the predecessor had seven: `improved` and `not better` were a GRADED answer, which nothing measures since a want is judged by its met-test and nothing scores a world by degree; `refused` was the legality check, which is the gates'; `remembered` was a plan adopted without a search. Each comes back with the thing that could return it." .

planning:Satisfied a planning:Outcome ; rdfs:label "satisfied" ;
    rdfs:comment "A world was reached where the want's met-test passes. The steps are the way there, and there are none where the want was already met at the root." .

planning:NoCandidate a planning:Outcome ; rdfs:label "no candidate" ;
    rdfs:comment "No action this agent holds points at this want: the search was offered no candidate at all. EQUIP ME, and it is not the same finding as Exhausted — a planner reporting the two alike would send somebody to buy a pump when the problem is the budget." .

planning:Exhausted a planning:Outcome ; rdfs:label "exhausted" ;
    rdfs:comment "Levers exist and no bounded sequence of them lands inside the region: the budget in worlds ran out, or the frontier emptied. MY DOSES ARE TOO COARSE, or my region is too tight for them." .

planning:for a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "for" ;
    rdfs:comment "What this was worked out FOR: the want a plan was found for, or the want or DESIRE a weighing was made for. NO RANGE, because a desire is not a want and both are weighed — a desire in every ground, a want in every world its search reaches — and NO DOMAIN, because a PLAN and a WEIGHING both say it. It was `forWant`, and the name lied the moment a desire was weighed. A plan's is the one thing about a plan execution has no word for, because the intentions keep commitments and not the reasoning that produced them; and it was declared twice with a domain each in the predecessor, which in RDFS is the intersection of the two: a property nothing could legally carry. A plan is named for its want and replaced whole, so a second pass over the same want leaves one plan and not two — and a reader that holds a want asks for its plan by this rather than by the name, which is a convention." .

#################  Desires, wants and the searched half of an action — planning's  #################

planning:Desire
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Desire" ;
    rdfs:comment """One thing an agent stands for, for its whole life: a node carrying the met-test (planning:metWhen, a SHACL shape, unchanged in content from when the desire WAS that shape) and a label a dashboard or the ask channel can print. It holds at every instant, it is NEVER handed to a search, and what is pursued is an planning:Want.

IT STATES NO TIME SEMANTICS, and neither does a want. A desire holds always, which its type says; a want holds over its graph's period, which the period says; a want that must hold AT an instant carries planning:holdsAt, which is the one thing a period cannot say. There was a binding property carrying all of that beside them, and every reader of it was asking which KIND or which FAMILY a node was (a-kind-is-a-type-not-a-binding, #681).

What the node does NOT carry is a measure of how badly it is unmet, and nothing else does either: a capability answered that through the choir, of whichever world was being judged, and a want is judged by its met-test now — see a-want-is-judged-by-its-met-test-and-nothing-else.""" .


planning:DesireGraph
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ;
    rdfs:label "Desire graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "A graph of `planning:Desire` rows — named for what it HOLDS, as every graph class is (a-graph-class-is-named-for-what-it-holds). A world authors one per agent, `<> a planning:DesireGraph`, with no period, so it holds at every instant as the T-Box does." .



planning:Want
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Want" ;
    rdfs:comment """What a desire comes to when a situation makes it bite: deduced FROM one — `prov:wasDerivedFrom` names which — bound to the instance the situation gives it, holding during a period of its own, and withdrawn when it is met. A desire stands and is never handed to a search; a want is the only thing that ever is. \"No unpaid debts\" is a desire, and a debt arriving deduces the want that closes it.

NOT A SUBCLASS OF planning:Desire, and it was one until the axis was measured. The entailment is materialised ONCE at genesis, and a want is minted long after — so nothing ever entailed the supertype for a runtime want and every writer hand-wrote `a planning:Want , planning:Desire` to compensate. The subclass bought nothing at runtime, cost a doubled type on every mint, and made `?d a planning:Desire` match both kinds, which is why a collection of desires had to filter on a binding to find its own contents. Unlinked, each type means exactly itself and no query filters (a-kind-is-a-type-not-a-binding).

HOW IT CAME TO BE IS NOT THE AXIS. Most wants are derived — `prov:wasDerivedFrom` names the desire — but a world may ratify one directly, and three do: hanoi's every disk home, the courier's every parcel delivered, the tower's. Those are wants, authored rather than deduced, and they are handed to a search like any other. Standing versus occasioned is the axis; who wrote it is provenance.

NOT a second modality and not a second store. Both live in the desire modality; what differs is which graph class holds them — `planning:DesireGraph` for the desires, holding at every instant, and `planning:WantGraph` for one graph per want, each with its period (#618, #645). See knowledge/domain/planning/desire.md.""" .


planning:WantGraph
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ;
    rdfs:label "Want graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "A graph of `planning:Want` rows. The derivation's are these, one per want it minted, each holding during its period and `orexis:arrivedBy orexis:Derived`; a world that states a want directly writes one `orexis:Asserted`, and a refinement's is the agent's own, `orexis:Recorded`. Which a reader means is the ARRIVAL axis, never a subclass. A want is not a desire, so this is no subclass of `planning:DesireGraph`." .


planning:Constraint
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Constraint" ;
    rdfs:comment """What a world says of itself is POSSIBLE, stated as a desire is stated — a node a holder `planning:holds`, carrying a shape under `planning:metWhen` or an avoided state under `planning:unmetWhen`, compiled by the same compiler into the same rows — and of a different MODALITY from a desire: a desire is bouletic and says what is wanted, a constraint alethic and says what can be. The dispatcher's is that no cell holds two vans, which is physics and not a preference.

A WORLD VIOLATING IT IS IMPOSSIBLE, NEVER REPAIRED. The search weighs every constraint of the holder whose footprint the scope's actions write in each possible world it weighs a want in, and a world where one yields a row is marked `planning:impossible` on its own row — off the frontier and never an achiever, whatever the want's met-test says there. No want is minted from a constraint: the derivation reads desires alone, and a constraint violated in the PRESENT is a contradiction between the world's state and its own word, said in the log by the pass and refused by `orexis-onboard` for a world posed that way, not a trouble the agent sets about mending. Not a `planning:Desire` and no subclass of one, since the one difference between them is the one that matters: an unmet desire is a want, and a violated constraint is a world that cannot be.

Its footprint is what couples: two wants whose plans a constraint could make collide are searched as one (`agent/planning/couplings.py`, one-mind-couples-the-wants-a-constraint-can-make-collide). A prohibition that is possible but forbidden — a state the agent may enter and must then leave — is not a constraint but an AVERSION, a desire in its `planning:unmetWhen` form, repaired when entered. See knowledge/domain/planning/constraint.md.""" .


planning:ConstraintGraph
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:PublicGraph ;
    rdfs:label "Constraint graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "A graph of `planning:Constraint` rows, with who holds each and the shape or avoided state each carries where no domain's shapes graph does — named for what it HOLDS, as every graph class is. A world authors one, `<> a planning:ConstraintGraph`, with no period: what is possible holds at every instant as the T-Box does. PUBLIC, not the agent's own, because what the world says is possible is everyone's — physics is not a belief one agent holds and another may lack — so it is read again at every boot as an ontology is, and an amended constraint reaches a running agent. The planner crosses it beside the graphs of desires, wants, records and shapes wherever it reads a met-test." .


planning:WantState
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Want state" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where a want has got to. `planning:Recognized` is the one value written: the want was minted and nothing has been planned yet. Where a plan stands is the plan's outcome and the intention's, and a met want is withdrawn rather than marked." .


planning:about
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain planning:Desire ;
    rdfs:label "about" ;
    rdfs:comment "What a want is ABOUT — the property a violation's constraint is about, carried from the met-test's report onto the want the derivation mints, and opaque to the kernel. A want about nothing in particular (a debt, a call) states none." .


planning:estimates
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain planning:Desire ;
    rdfs:label "what is left to pay" ;
    rdfs:comment "Points at a node carrying one `sh:select` that binds ?estimate — the remaining cost to meet this want, in the unit `planning:costs` is stated in, run over whichever world is being judged as the met-test is, and written on the weighing as `planning:remaining`. `$this` in the select is the instance: a desire's select leaves it unbound, a variable, and measures every instance; the derivation writes a want's own node with `$this` bound to the one instance the want is about, as it narrows the met-test, so a want per parcel reads its parcel's drives alone (#893). A want that declares none is simply not measurable this way, and the search treats every unmet world as equally far." .


planning:holds
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "holds" ;
    rdfs:comment "What this agent is held to or holds to: an `planning:Desire` (which carries its met-shape and its measure), a `planning:Constraint` (which carries a shape in the same words and says what is POSSIBLE rather than what is wanted), or a bare shape like the survival envelope, whose violation is a warning rather than a want. Replaces `orexis:boundedBy`, which named only the first and named it as though bounds were a kind of thing rather than a kind of FORCE. No `rdfs:range` since the desire was reified: a held thing is a desire, a constraint OR a shape, and a range naming one would quietly claim the others are not held." .


planning:holdsAt
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain planning:Desire ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "holds at" ;
    rdfs:comment "The instant a want must hold at — a predicted crossing: the start of the earliest ground at which its desire reads unmet. A want unmet in the present too is minted at none, since the present outranks the instant." .


planning:metWhen
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range sh:NodeShape ;
    rdfs:label "met when" ;
    rdfs:comment "The met-test: a SHACL node shape whose conformance says nothing is wanted here right now — met where every instance it targets conforms, and each violation an instance in trouble. Boolean by nature — it governs the outcome LABEL and a duty's discharge, never how hard to try. Never validated: compiled by `violation.report_select` into the select whose rows are its violations and run by `weigh` on the store's own engine over whichever world is judged. Its negative twin is `planning:unmetWhen`; a desire or a want carries one of the two, never both, and one carrying both is not judged at all (#892). NO DOMAIN: a desire, a want and a `planning:Constraint` all carry it, and a constraint carrying it is held to a shape that says what is POSSIBLE, not what is wanted — the same compiler, the same rows, a different modality. A domain naming the desire would have entailed that a constraint is one, which is the claim its page refuses." .


planning:state
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain planning:Want ;
    rdfs:range planning:WantState ;
    rdfs:label "state" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where this want has got to. On a WANT and never on a desire, because a desire has nowhere to get to — it stands for the agent's life and is either met or not at that instant." .


planning:unmetWhen
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "unmet when" ;
    rdfs:comment """The met-test's negative twin (#468; judged since #892): an aversion points here at the AVOIDED state — a node carrying one `sh:select`, SHACL-SPARQL's own form for a select whose rows are nodes — and is UNMET WHERE IT YIELDS A ROW. Ruled by the sovereign when the first build routed avoidance through `planning:metWhen`: a met-test pointing at the bad state evaluates right and READS inverted — 'met when dry-and-hot' — and a term that reads wrong to every skimmer is the flag-that-eventually-disagrees, in the reader's head. 'Unmet when two vans stand on one cell' reads as it evaluates.

The contract of the select: `SELECT $this ?value WHERE { … }` — `$this` the instance in trouble, `?value`, where the head projects it, the offending value the derivation keys a want by (the cell both vans stand on), and nothing else bound in. No `$state`: a text does not say which world it reads, and `weigh` hands it the world's graphs as the default graph exactly as it hands a met-test's compiled select. `planning:about` on the node says what the trouble is about, `sh:this` the instance itself, as a `sh:sparql` constraint may say it; `sh:targetNode` on the node, written by the derivation when it narrows a want to its instance, holds the rows to that instance. Compiled by `violation.entered_select` into the report's shape — no negation, one way of failing — and run by `weigh` in every ground as a met-test is, one `planning:violation` per row, so the derivation mints a want from an aversion exactly as from a met-test's violations and the want carries the avoided state, narrowed, under this same term. A select that will not compile or run writes no verdict, which the derivation reads as not judged and the search as unmet. Judged like any met-test: met or not, and nothing in between. NO DOMAIN, as `planning:metWhen` has none: a `planning:Constraint` carries it too — the dispatcher's two vans on one cell — and there a row is not trouble to repair but a world that is not possible.""" .



#################  Individuals  #################



planning:Recognized
    a planning:WantState ;
    rdfs:label "recognized" ;
    rdfs:comment "Minted. Its desire's met-test read unmet for this instance, or a world ratified it; nothing has been planned yet." .


planning:ShapesGraph
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:PublicGraph ;
    rdfs:label "Shapes graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What a desire's met-test and estimate point at: the SHACL shapes a domain declares for what its words mean when wanted — delivered, solved — and the selects that say how far a world still is, in the unit its actions cost. SHACL's own notion of a shapes graph, typed so that the planner crosses these beside the graphs of desires, wants and records, which is where it reads a met-test, without crossing every vocabulary: all nine ontology graphs cost thirty times the crossing, measured. Public, since what a word means is everyone's; a domain ships one, and a world that authors a shape of its own may put it here or beside its desire." .


planning:precondition
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain orexis:Action ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "precondition" ;
    rdfs:comment "When an action may be taken, as a SELECT over the world binding one variable per parameter the action `orexis:takes`, named for the parameter's local part; `$me` is substituted by whoever asks. A row is a step the world admits: it exists only where every hop of the walk holds, which is why chaining needs no `requires` of its own. The facts a step's precondition read in the world it was planned from are the step's precondition too (knowledge/domain/planning/precondition.md)." .


planning:effect
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain orexis:Action ;
    rdfs:range planning:Effect ;
    rdfs:label "effect" ;
    rdfs:comment "What taking an action makes true and false of the world it is taken in: the one declaration a search plans on and the world is held to (an-effect-is-one-declaration)." .


planning:Effect
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Effect" ;
    rdfs:comment "An action's effect as rules, `sh:rule` to each, run by a search on the possible world a step makes and never on beliefs — which is why an effect may delete, where a revision only concludes. A rule is a `sh:SPARQLRule` with an `sh:construct`, whose result is ADDED, or with an `planning:update`, a `DELETE … WHERE` naming no graph, which is run against the new world with the world's graphs as its dataset. Rules are grouped by `sh:order` as SHACL groups them: every rule of one order reads the same world, its deletions are applied before its additions, and a later order reads what the earlier ones made." .


planning:update
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "update" ;
    rdfs:comment "On an effect's `sh:SPARQLRule`, where SHACL has only `sh:construct`: a `DELETE … WHERE` taking out of the world a step makes whatever stands in the place the step changes, which nobody can name in advance. It names no graph; the search runs it `WITH` the new world and `USING` every graph of it." .


planning:costs
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain orexis:Action ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "costs" ;
    rdfs:comment """A SELECT yielding `?cost`: what taking this act spends, in the wallet's unit — euros, the unit of account bids are already priced in. Declared by the package that owns the action, exactly as `planning:landsAfter` declares its timing and for the same single-source reason: a search that invented a price would manufacture findings, and nothing upstream may pin a price on a specific act — the cost shapes the RANKING and nothing else (#466, control-the-derivative).

ABSENCE IS FREE, and that is a statement rather than a gap — the reading an omitted effect and an omitted timing already get. A look's energy and a dose's spent litres are real and unpriced today; each is one declaration in its owning package's file the day a world cares.

WHERE IT RANKS: under urgency, lexicographically. Cost speaks only between candidates urgency cannot separate — same urgency, cheaper wins — because wellbeing and money are different currencies and a scalar that mixed them could rank but never explain. A plan no better than standing still stays refused however cheap it is.""" .


planning:landsAfter
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain orexis:Action ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "lands after" ;
    rdfs:comment """A SELECT yielding `?least` and `?most`: the fewest and the most seconds after the act until the WORLD CHANGE can show — a BAND, since an interval is how this project says it does not know and membership in one is crisp; a rule that knows exactly binds both to one figure (#596). A query and not a number, because the duration is a function of the act rather than a property of the lever, and its parameters are the effect's own, so one text answers for any act it could be asked about. A world reached by the step holds over the band summed along its path, and the step the plan carries says both ends: `execution:landsAt`, the earliest, from which the executor looks for the answer, and `execution:notAfter`, the latest, a patience past which it gives up.

NOUGHT TWICE is a real answer and the honest one for a look. Observing changes nothing about the world; what a sensing act delays is KNOWLEDGE, and that is the confirmation route's business rather than this one's. A rule that omitted this would be claiming immediacy by silence, so the shapes ask for it.

WHEN THE WORLD CAN SHOW IT is what this answers, since the executor holds the step to the present from its earliest landing and fails it a patience past its latest with no answer. Three terms separate an act from its evidence: delivery (when the valve stops, from the device's own calibration), absorption (the pot's, and stated in NO world today), and the next reading (the sensor's cadence). A dose's effect speaks the side sensing's rules conclude of a reading, so nothing can show a dose before the sensor reads again, and the dose declares the third term as the band it is: the next reading is due within a cadence of the act — nothing at the least, the cadence at the most — read off the world in SSN-System's words. Declared as nothing, it landed the instant it was taken and failed a patience later, while the reading that would have answered it was still nine minutes off; declared as the cadence alone, a reading that came sooner was not looked at. A market act lands when its round's window closes, exactly, which is the band's two ends agreeing. The missing middle term is still missing: a pot that absorbs slower than its probe reads would answer late, and somebody must say what these pots do before the code may pick a number.""" .

planning:refines a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain planning:Want ;
    rdfs:label "refines" ;
    rdfs:comment "The step a want keeps one level down: minted by `refine` when a rule the store holds concludes the facts the step predicts, whose met-test is that rule's WHERE with the step's facts bound. The executor waits on the step while a plan for this want stands, and the Planner withdraws the want once no intention stands at the step it refines." .
